#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192300 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "3f141210-8194-5346-9e2a-c47f98bb3443" :question/math-academy-id "e-10840" :question/problem "Compute $P\\left(X > 8 \\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n${5e^{-5x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$, then\n\n$$\nP(a < X < b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 8) &= P(8 < X < ∞) \\\\\n&= ∫_{8}^{∞}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{8}^{∞}5e^{-5x}dx \\\\\n&= [- e^{-5x}]_{8}^{∞} \\\\\n&= e^{-40}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192301 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd8455e1-46f3-5956-80ec-e6753cc005a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "d40a67bd-7d98-58fb-b4a1-7a556e91556a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10851" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n${(k)/((4x + 1)^{3}),\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nIn our case, for the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{k}{(4x+1)^3}\n$$\n\non the interval\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nthe second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{k}{(4x+1)^3} \\, \\textrm dx = 1\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{∞}(k)/((4x + 1)^{3})dx &= - [(k)/(8(4x + 1)^{2})]_{0}^{∞} \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{8} = 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad k = 8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192355 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "5f988175-cdd9-5ddf-94b5-3b995dc75fce" :question/math-academy-id "e-12329" :question/problem "${(1)/((x - 2)^{2}),\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 3, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\geq 3$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^{\\infty} f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n3. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x \\geq 3$, we have $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{(x-2)^2} \\geq 0$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$∫_{3}^{∞}f(x)dx|= ∫_{3}^{∞}(1)/((x - 2)^{2})dx; = ∫_{3}^{∞}(x - 2)^{-2}dx; =-\\frac{1}{x - 2} \\mid _{3}^{∞}; = - (0 - 1); = 1$.\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d852c985-cbe1-5f90-8568-29934c457ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190646} {:answer/id #uuid "06935761-edc1-5300-a537-ad9758b0de55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190647} {:answer/id #uuid "4feb4af0-61e9-5d19-b099-eccf50a1b899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190648} {:answer/id #uuid "50c8f9ce-a318-54ed-a572-a51798cbc440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190649} {:answer/id #uuid "67b2e705-fb9c-541b-8f07-7fb4f822d8e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67b2e705-fb9c-541b-8f07-7fb4f822d8e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190650} :answer-field/id #uuid "03002436-b7cf-5314-9723-b24d1886cdd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190645}] :question/id #uuid "e8f7202f-949a-5544-b1eb-75cc6a08bdaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-154696" :question/problem "$f(x) = {e^{1 - x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x) \\ge 0$ for all $x \\ge 1$\n2. $∫_{1}^{∞}f(x)dx = 1$\n3. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93ca28c7-c073-5b0b-8f87-21a719911816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-8/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192091} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3dca22-bcb2-5164-8131-6b67a668b9c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192092} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e31b99-85f0-5712-ab